<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Routines</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> First, I Sit Down.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: concept: Use gestures and drawings to link daily routines to classroom activities that are performed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use gestures to act out daily classroom routines (e.g., pretending to sit down when asked).<br>Can clarify instructions by asking for repetition when needed (e.g., saying 'Repeat, please' when a command is not heard or understood).<br>Can use drawings to illustrate daily classroom routines (e.g., drawing a picture of a student reading).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key vocabulary and concepts related to daily routines and classroom activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Routine Action Review" using Total Physical Response (TPR). Teacher says an action like "First, sit down!" and performs the action, encouraging students to imitate. Other actions: "Pick up your pen!", "Open your bag!", "Put your paper on the desk!" Students actively perform and say the phrases. Use flashcards for 'chair', 'desk', 'bag', 'pen', 'paper' to prompt memory.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to use gestures to explain words and how to ask for repetition. "When I say 'sit down,' I show this (teacher sits). When I say 'pick up,' I show this (teacher picks up a pen). If you don't hear me, you can say 'Repeat, please?' (teacher cups ear and says the phrase)." The teacher models the pronunciation of /p/ in 'pick' and /d/ in 'down' and exaggerates the letter sounds A, B, C, D, E using alphabet flashcards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher sits down) "Am I standing up or sitting down?" (Expected: "Sitting down!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher pretends to jump) "Is this 'pick up'?" (Expected: "No!"). (Teacher picks up a pen) "Is this 'pick up'?" (Expected: "Yes!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher whispers an instruction) "What do you say if you don't hear?" (Prompts: "Repeat, please!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the scenario "Our Routines."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, teacher-made "Classroom Routine Chart" with simple drawings and labels (e.g., "First, I sit down.", "Next, I pick up my pen."). The chart uses pictures representing actions like sitting, picking up a pen, putting a bag away, and drawing. Each picture is accompanied by a simple phrase and a number (Step 1, Step 2, etc.).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing daily routines: Use simple language to describe classroom routines with numbers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to "Step 1" on the chart. "What do you see first?" (Students identify the action, e.g., "Sit down!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where is 'pick up the paper'?" (Students point to the corresponding drawing on the chart).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says "Draw a picture." "Which picture shows 'draw'?" (Students point).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation, practicing target language in controlled and semi-controlled activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Imperatives (e.g., 'Sit down.', 'Pick it up.'), Sequencing words with numbers (e.g., 'Step 1, Step 2'), Vocabulary (chair, desk, bag, pen, paper, sit, pick up, draw, Please).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Action & Word Match" (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher says an imperative (e.g., "Please, sit down!"). Students perform the action. Then, the teacher shows a flashcard of an action (e.g., picking up a pen), and students say the corresponding phrase ("Pick up the pen!"). The teacher emphasizes /p/ and /d/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Draw Your Routine" (Semi-controlled):</b> Students receive a piece of paper. The teacher calls out a simple routine step (e.g., "First, I sit down." or "Pick up your bag."). Students draw a simple picture representing that action. After drawing, each student holds up their picture and says the routine step. This is a mediation activity where they link their drawing to the spoken phrase.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Explain Your Action" (Pair Interaction / Peer Mediation):</b> In pairs, one student performs a routine action (e.g., pretends to open a notebook). Their partner tries to guess and say the correct phrase (e.g., "Open your notebook, please!"). If the partner doesn't understand the action, the first student can use more gestures or say the phrase again. If the partner doesn't hear, they can practice saying "Repeat, please?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario, focusing on the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task:</b> "My Routine Chart Mediation Presentation." Students will present their individual "Routine Chart" (the 21st Century Skills Project) to a small group of peers or to the teacher. They will use their chart, gestures, and simple English words to *mediate* the meaning of each routine step. The chart includes labels like 'First, I sit down.' and 'Pick it up.' for different activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Each student, with their "Routine Chart," stands in front of a small group. They point to each drawing on their chart, explain it with a simple phrase (e.g., "Step 1: First, I sit down."), and simultaneously act out the routine using gestures. They are encouraged to make eye contact and speak clearly. If a peer seems confused, the presenter uses more elaborate gestures or simplifies their language. The teacher circulates, observing and prompting. For example, the teacher might pretend not to hear an instruction to prompt the student to say, "Repeat, please?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in presenting the routine, effective use of gestures and drawings to clarify meaning (mediation), using target vocabulary and grammar, and communicative task completion (explaining the chart). Students demonstrating the ability to clarify instructions when needed.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of student learning and self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during the "Routine Chart Mediation Presentation."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Routine Chart" (21st Century Skills Project). They must use gestures to act out at least two routines, use their drawings to illustrate three routines, and verbally describe at least two routines using sequencing words (e.g., 'First, I sit down.') and target vocabulary. They will also be assessed on their ability to respond with "Repeat, please" if the teacher or a peer quietly asks them a question.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses gestures to act out daily classroom routines (e.g., 'sit down', 'pick up').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses drawings on the chart to illustrate daily classroom routines.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (sit, pick up, paper, pen, Please) and grammar (imperatives, sequencing words like 'First') intelligibly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in explaining the routine steps (can peers understand the mediated message?).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates asking for repetition when needed (e.g., saying 'Repeat, please').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students choose a smiley face for each question on a simple worksheet:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I use my hands to show 'sit down'?" (Yes/No with simple drawing options for smiley/sad face).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I say 'Repeat, please' if I didn't hear?" (Yes/No with simple drawing options for smiley/sad face).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Opportunity for students and teacher to reflect on the learning process and outcomes.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy about showing your routine chart with your hands?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was hard about explaining your chart to your friend using drawings and words?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully use gestures and drawings to link daily routines to classroom activities as outlined in the specific objective?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were they able to effectively mediate their routine charts to their peers, making the information accessible?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which students struggled with asking for repetition ("Repeat, please") and how can I provide more support for this pragmatic function in future lessons?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more visual prompts, peer modeling) are needed for future mediation tasks, especially for early learners?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes our theme on "Our Routines." In the next theme, we will build on our understanding of actions and instructions to explore new activities we can do in different parts of the school, continuing to use gestures and simple drawings to explain new concepts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>